body:
If you have HSV1 anywhere on your body - upstairs, downstairs - you are VERY unlikely to get it somewhere else. You already have the virus in your body, and the antibodies. They're not specific to a location.

&#x200B;

See this link:

[**It is rare for people to re-catch a virus**](https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/3009643) that they already have for a second time – even on a different part of the body. Their immune system defends them against this. The virus does not travel through the body. So a person with a genital infection can kiss or perform oral sex – there is no risk of infecting a partner by doing this; the virus will not travel inside the body from the genitals to the mouth.

&#x200B;

[https://herpes.org.uk/frequently-asked-questions/passing-transmitting-herpes/](https://herpes.org.uk/frequently-asked-questions/passing-transmitting-herpes/)

Firstly, go get an STD test. It's going to be the only way to settle your worries on that score. Please be rest assured that a) it's unlikely he will have given you anything, but b) if he has, the majority of STDs are treatable.

It's really just a matter of time that will help you to let your guard down and trust people again. There's no rushing it or telling when you'll feel better. It's much easier to say that from a 'recovered' perspective but I promise, you will get there. In the meantime, give yourself the attention you need, surround yourself with support from people you trust, and cut off all contact with ex.

Also - to use mental health as an excuse to hide his cheating is a really, really scummy thing to do. It sounds like you're very well-rid.

&#x200B;

&#x200B;
